  "raw_text": "Step 1: Rewrite the equation to isolate terms involving \\(x\\) on one side:\n\\[3^{2x} - 10^x = -19\\]\n\nStep 2: Recognize that \\(3^{2x} = (3^2)^x = 9^x\\), so rewrite the equation as:\n\\[9^x - 10^x = -19\\]\n\nStep 3: Let's test some values of \\(x\\) to see if we can find a solution. Start with \\(x = 2\\):\n\\[9^2 - 10^2 = 81 - 100 = -19\\]\nThis satisfies the equation.\n\nStep 4: Verify that there are no other solutions by considering the behavior of the functions \\(9^x\\) and \\(10^x\\). Since \\(10^x\\) grows faster than \\(9^x\\), the difference \\(9^x - 10^x\\) will be negative for \\(x > 2\\) and positive for \\(x < 2\\). Therefore, \\(x = 2\\) is the only solution.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{2}<|im_end|>",
